bodyText:
New Mexico law requires tenants to:
- follow housing-code duties that affect health and safety;
- keep their part of the premises as clean and safe as its condition permits and leave it as clean as at move-in, except for ordinary wear and tear;
- dispose of waste safely;
- keep plumbing fixtures clean;
- use facilities and appliances reasonably;
- not deliberately or negligently damage the premises or knowingly let others do so;
- not disturb neighbors;
- follow condominium, cooperative or neighborhood association rules;
- and not knowingly commit or allow a substantial violation.

A tenant who intentionally damages the premises is liable for two times the monthly rent.

New Mexico says notice by notice when it must be in writing, and a lease cannot replace a required written notice with a call, a conversation or a text message.

From you to the tenant, these must be written:
- the lease itself, before occupancy;
- the manager and owner disclosure, at or before move-in;
- notice of a rent increase (30 days) or a fee increase (60 days);
- notice of entry (24 hours, stating the purpose, date and estimated time frame);
- the 7-day notice for a lease breach, the 3-day notice for unpaid rent and the 3-day notice for a substantial violation;
- notice ending a week-to-week or month-to-month tenancy;
- the itemized deposit statement within 30 days;
- notice that your interest in the property or your management has ended;
- notice of intent to dispose of property left after abandonment;
- and, for applicants, notice of the screening fee (written or digital).

From the tenant to you, these must be written:
- notice of your failure to make repairs, before the tenant may abate rent or end the lease;
- notice ending the lease if you don't deliver possession;
- notice within seven days of ending the lease after a fire or casualty;
- and notice ending a periodic tenancy.

Some notices the act requires are not said to be written, such as the late-fee notice, notice of new or amended rules, and the tenant's notice of an extended absence; give and ask for those in writing anyway, so you can prove them. Written includes printed or any other way of representing words. An e-mail can count as a writing only where both of you agreed to deal electronically, and never for a notice of default, eviction or the right to cure for a tenant's primary residence.

How each written notice must be delivered is in "How to Serve Notices in New Mexico"; optional e-mail use is in "E-mail Communications" clause.
